The attached image (Figure 6 from the PDF) illustrates how Nano-11 improves antigen targeting and retention. Panel A shows that migratory dendritic cells (MigDC) in the draining lymph node express the highest levels of the costimulatory molecule CD86 among antigen-presenting cells. Panel B further phenotypes OVA-positive and OVA-negative MigDCs based on CD11b and Ly6C expression. Panel C demonstrates in vivo retention of fluorescently labeled OVA. It shows that OVA adjuvanted with Nano-11 (OVA+N11) or aluminum hydroxide (OVA+AH) remains at the injection site longer than soluble OVA over seven days, indicating Nano-11's ability to prolong antigen availability.

The image can significantly contribute to vaccine development by showcasing Nano-11's capability as a potent adjuvant. Its ability to effectively target antigens to migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs), which are crucial for initiating adaptive immune responses, and to prolong antigen retention at the injection site provides a basis for designing more effective and long-lasting vaccines. This mechanism-based evidence supports Nano-11's potential in developing next-generation prophylactic vaccines.